<body>
    <h1>IIQ Sailpoint Integration</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#333333;">II=
Q is the system that manages internal user access within NBC. However, acce=
ss to CompassX for internal users is handled directly within FUM and is not=
 synchronized with IIQ. According to the bank=E2=80=99s access management p=
olicy (IP 520-010), an access matrix must be documented and approved based =
on the latest model validated by the Identity and Access Management (IAM) t=
eam. The new CompassX application has been identified as a critical asset, =
and currently, its user list is not integrated into the SailPoint IIQ tool.=
 Integration is required to enable necessary checks, such as monitoring use=
r departures.</span></span></p>
<p>To support this initiative, IIQ should be integrated with CompassX to ma=
nage internal user access. Currently, both external and internal user acces=
s to CompassX is handled by FUM. After integration, internal user access pr=
ovisioning will move to IIQ, while external user access will continue to be=
 managed by FUM.</p>
<p>However, need to determine the level of access provisioning that can be =
handled by IIQ. There are two options:</p>
<ul>
 <li>
  <p><strong>Basic Access</strong>: IIQ manages onboarding details such as =
employee ID, name, preferences, and CompassX access levels (e.g., view, edi=
t, moderator, admin). FUM continues to manage fine-grained access control f=
or CompassX features.<br><em>Pros</em>: IIQ remains the provisioning system=
 for internal users. No need to sync frequently changing fine-grained acces=
s profiles between IIQ and CompassX.<br><em>Cons</em>: SAAs must still log =
into both systems to provide complete access.</p></li>
 <li>
  <p><strong>Full Access</strong>: CompassX passes all fine-grained access =
control provisions to IIQ, allowing SAAs to define complete access during o=
nboarding.<br><em>Pros</em>: A single system manages internal user access.<=
br><em>Cons</em>: Requires more integration and real-time syncing of access=
 policies.</p></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>It was decided to adopt the basic access(<span style=3D"color:#1=
72b4d;">Coarse-grained access)</span> option for this implementation by bus=
iness.</strong></p>
<p><br></p>
<p>This is the first step in Employee Tenant migration and this change woul=
d enable IIQ to start with their activities where as CompassX changes for e=
mployee tenant migration will start early 2026.&nbsp;</p>
<p><br></p>
<p><span style=3D"color:#172b4d;"><span style=3D"color:#333333;"><img class=
=3D"drawio-diagram-image" width=3D"468" style=3D"width:600px;max-width: 100=
%;" src=3D"12859690a934b3f3461aad178698840efc5f2f1132985e8855131892716c2ecf=
" height=3D"264"></span></span></p>
